  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Show that $5^{2n+1}+6^{2n+1}$ is divisible by 11 for every natural number $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$5^{2n+1}+6^{2n+1}\\equiv0\\pmod{11}$.\n\nUse $6\\equiv-5\\pmod{11}$ and the fact that $2n+1$ is odd.",
      "worked": "Since $6\\equiv-5\\pmod{11}$ and $2n+1$ is odd,\n$$\\begin{align*}5^{2n+1}+6^{2n+1}&\\equiv5^{2n+1}+(-5)^{2n+1}\\pmod{11}\\\\5^{2n+1}+6^{2n+1}&\\equiv5^{2n+1}-5^{2n+1}\\pmod{11}\\\\5^{2n+1}+6^{2n+1}&\\equiv0\\pmod{11}.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $5^{2n+1}+6^{2n+1}$ is divisible by 11 for every natural number $n$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
